"use strict";(()=>{function t(e){return typeof window<"u"&&window.__rvLesson&&window.__rvLesson(e),e}var s=t({id:0,title:"Warm-up and vocabulary",short:"Warm-up",goal:'Combinational versus sequential logic, the clock edge, and what "single cycle" means.',level:null,scope:[],steps:[{title:"What we will build",text:`Over the next lessons you will build a processor that runs the RV32I base instruction set: 37 instructions covering arithmetic, logic, loads, stores, branches and jumps. ecall, ebreak, fence and the CSR instructions are out of scope.

The circuit starts almost empty and grows one block at a time. Each block appears only when an instruction needs it.`,panel:"dp-vs-ctrl"},{title:"Combinational logic",text:"A combinational circuit has no memory: its outputs are a function of its present inputs only. Adders, multiplexers, decoders and the ALU are combinational. When an input changes, the output changes after a propagation delay.",panel:"comb-seq"},{title:"Sequential logic",text:"A sequential circuit stores state. A register is a group of edge-triggered D flip-flops: it copies its D input to its output Q only at the rising clock edge and holds Q steady in between. In this processor the PC, the register file and the data memory hold all the state.",panel:"comb-seq"},{title:"The clock edge",text:"All state elements share one clock. Between edges, combinational logic computes new values from the stored state; at the edge, every state element captures its input at the same instant. This discipline makes the design predictable.",panel:"clock"},{title:"Setup and hold, at a high level",text:"A flip-flop only captures D reliably if D is stable for a short setup time before the edge and a hold time after it. So the clock period must be long enough for the slowest combinational path to settle, plus the setup time.",panel:"setup-hold"},{title:"Datapath and control path",text:"The datapath is the plumbing that moves and transforms 32-bit values. The control path looks at the instruction and produces small control signals that steer the datapath: mux selects, the ALU operation, write enables.",panel:"dp-vs-ctrl"},{title:'What "single cycle" means',text:"In a single-cycle processor each instruction runs completely within one clock cycle: fetch, decode, read registers, compute, access memory, write back, and choose the next PC. The steps in this course show the order in which signals become valid inside that one cycle; registers and memory change only at the closing clock edge.",panel:"single-cycle"},{title:"Notation used throughout",text:"Registers are x0 to x31; `R[rs1]` means the value in register rs1 and `M[a]` means memory at byte address a. Bit ranges are written [hi:lo] (bits 19:15 are five bits) and `0x` marks hexadecimal. Instruction fields always use the same colours, {opcode} {rd} {funct3} {rs1} {rs2} {funct7} {imm}, and control signals look like <ctl>RegWrite</ctl>.",panel:"single-cycle"}],practice:[{kind:"quiz",title:"Vocabulary check",concepts:["comb-vs-seq","clock-edge","setup-hold","single-cycle"]}]});})();
